	url = {http://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/7795340/},
	doi = {10.1109/MILCOM.2016.7795340},
	abstract = {Cyber-physical critical infrastructures have been a frequent target of cyber-attacks due to the interconnected setup of networked devices that are utilized with digitally controlled physical systems. In this study, we aim to examining the impact of cyber-attacks that inﬂict various time delays on a SCADA system. In order to demonstrate this issue, a cyber-physical testbed emulating a power generation station has been built. The testbed incorporates physical components simulating power units, realtime programmable logic controllers (PLCs), sensors, actuators, and network devices. To simulate exposing a time delay attack over the network, the Dummynet kernel module is used. Then, time delays of varying lengths are injected into the controlled network. Our experimental results show that a time delay variation attack can outstandingly affect control system performance and may jeopardize control system mission success and cause the system to crash.},
